<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Clan Sinclair</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Clan Sinclair is a Scottish clan of Norman origin based in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caithness">Caithness</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkney</a>. In 1379 Henry Sinclair was invested as Earl of Orkney by the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, and his descendants held the earldom until William Sinclair gave it up to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-iii-of-scotland">James III of Scotland</a> in 1470. The family later held the earldom of Caithness and built Rosslyn Chapel.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/clan-sinclair.jpg" alt="Clan Sinclair" width="320" height="407" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Celtus · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Clan_member_crest_badge_-_Clan_Sinclair.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name, de Sancto Claro in French and Sanctus Clarus in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, derives from the hermit St. Clare and from St. Clair-sur-Epte near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. No certain record exists, but the family probably came from Saint-Clair in Normandy and reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William the Conqueror</a>; Richard and Brittel of Saint-Clair appear in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domesday-book">Domesday Book</a>. William of Saint-Clair is said to have escorted Saint Margaret to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> in 1068 and to have been granted the barony of Roslin in return.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>William St. Clair commanded a wing of Alexander III's army against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haakon-iv-of-norway">Haakon IV of Norway</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-largs">Battle of Largs</a> in 1263. In the Wars of Scottish Independence Sir William Sinclair of Rosslyn was captured at Dunbar in 1296, while Henry Sinclair of Rosslyn helped defeat the English at Roslin in 1303 and, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-the-bruce">Robert the Bruce</a>, at Loudoun Hill in 1307. The clan fought for Bruce at Bannockburn in 1314, and Sir William Sinclair was among the Scots killed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-douglas-governor">Sir James Douglas</a> at Teba in 1330.</p><p>William Sinclair, 1st Earl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caithness">Caithness</a>, High Chancellor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> from 1454 to 1458, received Caithness in 1455 and divided the family lands, passing over his eldest son, William the Waster, who became Lord Sinclair. William Sinclair, 2nd Earl of Caithness, was killed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-flodden">Battle of Flodden</a> in 1513 with 300 Sinclairs, and in the 16th century the clan feuded with the Clan Sutherland. A dispute with Sir John Campbell of Glenorchy over the earldom led to the Battle of Altimarlach, but in 1681 Parliament restored it to the Sinclairs.</p><p>The clan supported the Jacobites in 1715. In 1745 many Sinclairs had Jacobite sympathies, but their chief, the Earl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caithness">Caithness</a>, backed the government; only about thirty men under Sinclair of Scotscalder answered the call of the Jacobite Lord MacLeod, and Sir James Sinclair of Rosslyn commanded the Royal Scots for the government at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-culloden">Culloden</a> in 1746.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Besides Rosslyn Chapel, the Sinclairs built or held Roslin Castle, their earliest seat in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, Castle Sinclair Girnigoe near Wick, the Castle of Mey, sold in 1952 to Queen Elizabeth The Queen Mother, and Ravenscraig Castle in Kirkcaldy, one of the first in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> built for artillery. The Sinclairs of Roslin claimed to be hereditary Grand Master Masons of Scotland, and William St Clair became the first elected Grand Master of the Grand Lodge of Scotland.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Clan Sinclair” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the Norse line died out in 1231, Scottish families held the earldom, ending with the <b>Sinclairs</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/earldom-of-orkney">Earldom of Orkney</a></li></ul></section>
